Gitcha starts only when asked to.

Packages built from debian/ (dpkg-buildpackage, deploy/build-debian.sh) ask with debconf for the public
URL, the hosting domains and the database, and whether to start now; change the answers with
  dpkg-reconfigure inputloop-gitcha
They go to /etc/inputloop/gitcha/debconf.env, which the unit reads after gitcha.env.  For many hosts at
once, answer them in a preseed file: /usr/share/doc/inputloop-gitcha/preseed.example.cfg.

By hand (and for packages built with deploy/build-debs.sh, which ask nothing):

  1. Edit /etc/inputloop/gitcha/gitcha.env (public URL, hosting domains, the database, and so on).
  2. systemctl enable --now gitcha        (listens on 127.0.0.1:4873 only)
  3. Put a TLS proxy in front: /usr/share/doc/inputloop-gitcha/Caddyfile.example.
  4. For git over SSH: /usr/share/doc/inputloop-gitcha/sshd-gitcha.conf.example.

The database.  GITCHA_STORE=cassandra (the default, unless the package was built without Cassandra)
keeps it in an Apache Cassandra keyspace: install Cassandra from https://cassandra.apache.org (its apt
repository; it needs Java 17), or point GITCHA_CASSANDRA_CONTACT_POINTS at an existing cluster.
GITCHA_STORE=file keeps it in /var/lib/gitcha/db.json.  An empty Cassandra instance that finds a
db.json imports it once and renames it db.json.imported-to-cassandra.

Everything else (git repositories, sites, blobs, logs, and the keys it encrypts with) is /var/lib/gitcha.
Back up the database (db.json, or the keyspace with nodetool snapshot) and the repositories; keep
secret.key apart from those backups.  DEPLOY.md, beside this file, is the full guide.
